\newcommand{\RloadCy}{R}
For Load A only, voltage and current use the passive sign convention and the measured resistance is constant.
\[V=I\RloadCy\]

\newcommand{\RloadCy}{R}
In a separate fixture subreport, Load B has an independent local binding rendered as the same glyph. Its branch data obey
\[V_b=I_b\RloadCy\]

\newcommand{\TgasCy}{T}
The sealed vessel is at one equilibrium state and the listed gas is ideal.
\[PV=nR\TgasCy\]
\input{audit}

\newcommand{\CfixedCy}{C}
Assume an ideal capacitor with constant capacitance and a differentiable terminal voltage \(V(t)\). Current is positive into the positive-voltage terminal.
\[I=\CfixedCy\frac{dV}{dt}\]

\newcommand{\RhoTankCy}{\rho}
The liquid is at rest, but sparse salinity measurements do not establish whether its density is constant with depth. The worksheet provisionally proposes the pressure difference
\[p=\RhoTankCy gh\]

\newcommand{\ViscCy}{\mu}
The logger exports a column called viscosity but its metadata does not say whether the value is dynamic viscosity or kinematic viscosity. The reduction uses
\[\mathrm{Re}=\frac{\rho vL}{\ViscCy}\]

\newcommand{\EplateCy}{E}
A parallel-plate assembly produces a uniform electric field at the bead. No magnetic field or other applied field is present. This field exerts the force
\[F=q\EplateCy\]

\newcommand{\MassBodyCy}{m}
A cart and a puck remain in contact while the force sensor is sampled; both bodies can accelerate and their masses differ. The report states that \(F\) is the net force on whichever body its next phrase selects, but then says only ``for this body,'' and writes
\[F=\MassBodyCy a\]

\newcommand{\SpeedCarriageCy}{v}
The report gives an actuator force vector and then a carriage velocity vector. It calls the latter the operating speed, but does not state that the two vectors are collinear or provide their angle. It reports scalar power as
\[P=F\SpeedCarriageCy\]

\newcommand{\RsharedCy}{R}
The dossier explicitly declares one scalar \(R\), with one numerical value and no conversion, to be both the heater's electrical resistance and its sink's thermal resistance.
\[V=I\RsharedCy\]
\input{thermal}

\newcommand{\QpipeCy}{Q}
For fully developed laminar flow of a Newtonian liquid through a circular tube, the sizing sheet uses the Hagen--Poiseuille result
\[\QpipeCy=\frac{\pi r^{4}\Delta p}{8\mu L}\]

\newcommand{\ReditCy}{R}
For the fixed resistor under the passive sign convention,
\[V=I\ReditCy\]

\newcommand{\SpeedDraftCy}{c}
The first draft treats a nondispersive wave and states
\[\SpeedDraftCy=f\lambda\]

\newcommand{\OmegaWaterCy}{\omega}
The revision removes the nondispersive model and instead treats finite-depth surface gravity waves, with gravitational acceleration \(g\), wave number \(k\), and water depth \(h\):
\[\OmegaWaterCy^{2}=gk\tanh(kh)\]

\newcommand{\FluxDraftCy}{q}
The draft models one-dimensional conduction through insulation:
\[\FluxDraftCy=-k\nabla T\]

\newcommand{\QradCy}{\dot{Q}}
The revision removes the conduction equation and estimates only net gray-surface radiation between a surface at \(T_s\) and large surroundings at \(T_\infty\):
\[\QradCy=\varepsilon\sigma A\left(T_s^{4}-T_\infty^{4}\right)\]
